{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChFYkXVVAkcGoJDivC91T","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[x] With a pre-existing target directory that has no manifest, `install` exits non-zero and leaves it untouched\n[x] With that foreign directory, `install --force` installs\n\nAll four checklist items are done and committed. The full `npm test` run passes: 10 Node tests plus the Python suite.\n\n`install` now checks the target directory before copying anything. If there's no directory or it's empty, the install goes ahead. If the directory exists but has no manifest from this package, it counts as foreign. If any installed file's hash differs from the last manifest, that's a local edit. In either of the last two cases, `install` lists the problem, exits 1 and changes nothing: it doesn't even run `hermes plugins validate`. `--force` overwrites anyway.\n\nThe comparison lives in `src/manifest.ts` so `doctor` (ticket 10) can reuse it. `readManifest` returns nothing if the manifest is missing, unreadable, or written by another package. `compareManifest` returns two lists: `edited` (hash differs) and `missing` (in the manifest but gone). A deleted file doesn't block a re-install, because there's no edit to lose.\n\n1. The refusal check was written in the first slice, so the tests for the foreign directory and for `--force` passed as soon as I wrote them. To make sure they really test something, I disabled the relevant code briefly and confirmed each one then failed.\n2. `install` now rejects options it doesn't recognise. Whoever adds `--configure`/`--no-configure` has to add them to the allowed list in `src/cli.ts`. I noted this in `AGENTS.md`.\n3. `install --force` over a foreign directory leaves behind files that aren't part of the package. Removing stale files isn't in this ticket.\n\nNotes for this attempt are in `.redeye/notes.md`, which is not committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequen